# TLS termination and the only public entrance. Caddy provisions and renews
# the certificate itself via ACME; port 80 exists for the challenge and the
# HTTPS redirect. gRPC-Web is plain HTTP POST underneath, so an ordinary
# reverse proxy is all the transport needs.
#
# Every hostname below must resolve before first boot, or certificate issuance
# fails and retries with backoff — one certificate per site block, so a name
# added here whose record has not landed holds the whole set back. Those records
# are applied from infra/main.tf, so what is actually being waited on is the NS
# delegation at the registrar.
#
# The names are the same literals as the Route53 zone and records in
# infra/main.tf. Nothing renders this file — deploy:api rsyncs it as-is — so the
# two sides are kept in step by hand, and both say so.
# Caddy 2.7 made HTTP metrics opt-in, and the failure without this block is the
# quiet kind: the endpoint below answers, Prometheus records the target as up,
# and the exposition contains configuration metrics and not one request. Checked
# rather than assumed — a Caddy without this line emits zero
# `caddy_http_requests_total`.
{
	servers {
		metrics
	}
}

# Caddy's own metrics, on the compose network and nowhere else — no host port
# publishes 2019, so only Prometheus beside it can connect.
#
# A site block rather than `admin :2019`. Caddy serves the same metrics from its
# admin endpoint, but that endpoint also accepts configuration writes: opening it
# to the compose network would mean any container on it could rewrite the reverse
# proxy, which is a large thing to hand over in exchange for a scrape. The
# `metrics` directive serves the exposition and nothing else.
:2019 {
	metrics
}

# The API and the marketing page are split by hostname, not by path. Both names
# resolve to this one box today; the split is what lets that stop being true
# without an App Store release, since the app can only ever reach the host it
# was compiled with. infra/main.tf carries the longer form of the argument on
# the record itself.
#
# Everything on this name is the API, so there is no path allowlist to keep in
# step with the contract and no file_server underneath to catch what misses.
# An unknown path here reaches tonic and gets its answer, rather than a 200
# carrying an HTML page — which is the routing regression the Route 53 probe
# had to match on the body to catch.
#
# /grpc.reflection.* needs no exclusion for the same reason: crates/api/src/grpc.rs
# registers reflection on local environments only, so on this box the service
# is simply absent and tonic says so. Worth stating because an unset OND_ENV
# falls back to dev — the gate in the binary fails open, and what closes it
# here is that the request reaches a server which never registered the service.
#
# No rate limiting, deliberately: Caddy's is an xcaddy plugin, and building a
# custom image for it would mean a second `docker save | ssh docker load` leg
# in a deploy that has one of each. The ration lives in
# crates/api/src/throttle.rs instead, which is also what lets tests/e2e drive
# it. What this proxy does contribute is the key that limiter counts against:
# reverse_proxy appends the peer it accepted to X-Forwarded-For, and the
# limiter reads that right-most entry.
api.ondbreathe.app {
	reverse_proxy api:18100
}

ondbreathe.app {
	root * /srv/web

	encode zstd gzip

	# Extensionless URLs, because the app ships them as literals:
	# `LegalLinks.privacyPolicy` is `https://ondbreathe.app/privacy`, and App
	# Review rejects a paywall whose privacy link 404s. `file_server` alone
	# resolves neither `/privacy` nor `/support`.
	try_files {path} {path}.html {path}/index.html

	file_server
}
